# Agent notes for somewm development

Operational knowledge that CONTRIBUTING.md does not cover. Read this before
launching test instances or debugging IPC.

## What this tree is

somewm is a Wayland-native reimplementation of AwesomeWM (X11-only): a new
project with compatible Lua APIs, not a fork of AwesomeWM. Upstream is
`github.com/trip-zip/somewm` (remote `origin`), maintained by Jimmy Cozza.

This tree is the user's personal repo. The active personal stack is
`perf/2026.10oct.30-upstream-fold` — upstream plus the user's own fixes
and features folded onto the node-model upstream. The repo has two
linked worktrees sharing one object store:

- `~/src/somewm` — the main worktree, on `draft/test-builds`: the
  scratch line for building test binaries. Check out whatever needs
  building here; commits on it do not move the session branch.
- `~/src/somewm-next` — a linked worktree that alone holds
  `perf/2026.10oct.30-upstream-fold`, the confirmed-working branch the
  live session builds and runs (see below). A branch can only be
  checked out in one worktree, so scratch work cannot ride onto the
  session branch by accident.

(`~/src/somewm-next` was a separate clone with a rewritten history until
2026-11-03, converted to a worktree after its tree was verified identical
to this repo's tip; renamed from `~/src/somewm-plus` on 2026-11-02. The
old `combined/main` tip stays pinned as `old-combined-main` here and
`combined/main` on `fork`.) `~/src/somewm-fixed` holds
`feature/2026.10oct.29-personal-layer`, a squashed reference of the same
layer. The local `combined/main` branch is a retired mirror of the perf
stack — the `plus` remote that fed it was the old clone path and was
removed with the conversion, so it no longer refreshes. The `fork`
remote is the user's GitHub mirror; the perf branch tracks
`fork/perf/2026.10oct.30-upstream-fold`, so a plain `git push` in
`somewm-next` mirrors it.

The perf branch shares ancestry with upstream, so `origin/main..HEAD`
counts are honest. The retired branches forked before the tree rewrite:
`git rev-list --count old-combined-main...origin/main` reports hundreds
behind while the real gap is a handful of commits. Judge those by
subject (`git log --format=%s A..B` on both sides), never by SHA.

## The live compositor builds from ~/src/somewm-next

`~/bin/somewm` (a dotfiles wrapper script) runs `~/src/somewm-next`'s
binary with `-L ~/src/somewm-next/lua`, rebuilding it with ninja when
the tree is stale. Two builds of the same tree exist side by side:
`build-release` (optimized, the default session) and `build-asan`
(ASan/UBSan + bench, for crash-hunting). The wrapper does not `exec`:
a run dying inside 120s retries once, then boots the promoted snapshot
`~/.local/share/somewm-good/{somewm,lua}` (binary + matching lua tree,
frozen by `~/bin/somewm-promote [build-dir]`, default build-release).
Three greeter entries ride on this: `somewm` (release/edge), `somewm
(asan)` and `somewm (good)` — each Exec's `somewm-session-variant
<edge|asan|good>` (`~/bin`), which starts `somewm@<variant>.service`
(from the `somewm@.service` template in `~/.config/systemd/user/`,
ExecStart=`~/bin/somewm %i`). The wrapper dispatches on $1, else
`SOMEWM_BUILD`, else edge; `somewm-session-asan`/`-good` remain as
thin compat shims. `somewm.service` + its `somewm.service.d/` drop-ins
stay as the stock fallback path (ExecStart=~/bin/somewm → edge).
Shared env drop-ins live in `somewm@.service.d/`. Check which binary a
running session is on with `readlink /proc/$(pgrep -x somewm)/exe`.

`~/src/somewm-next` alone holds `perf/2026.10oct.30-upstream-fold`, the
confirmed-working branch — work lands directly on it (or merges in from
a topic branch), so patching the `somewm-next` source is the normal
flow. Anything not meant for the session belongs on `~/src/somewm`'s
own branch (`draft/test-builds` or a topic checkout there).

- Lua edits: `ninja -C build somewm` is not needed; `awesome.restart()`
  (hot-reload) re-reads rc.lua in the same process — but the live
  process reads `somewm-next/lua`, so the change must land in that tree
  to take effect.
- C edits: `ninja -C ~/src/somewm-next/build-release somewm` (or
  `build-asan` for the instrumented run), then a **real** restart —
  `systemctl --user restart somewm` re-execs the new binary but kills
  all Wayland clients (there is no seamless re-exec). `awesome.restart()`
  does NOT pick up C changes. Never restart without the user's say-so.
- Verify the live binary matches before debugging runtime behaviour:
  `ps -o lstart= -p $(pgrep -x somewm)` vs
  `stat -c %y ~/src/somewm-next/build-asan/somewm`.

## Nested test instances and the IPC socket

`ipc_init()` (ipc.c) picks its socket path as:

1. `SOMEWM_SOCKET` if set
2. otherwise `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/somewm-socket` — the **main session's path**

Historically `ipc_init` unconditionally `unlink()`ed that path before
`bind()`, so a nested instance launched without `SOMEWM_SOCKET` stole the
running compositor's IPC socket: the socket file was unlinked and rebound,
the main compositor's listener stayed open but unreachable, and
`somewm-client` silently talked to the headless test instance (a dead
giveaway: `screens=0` on a session with real outputs).

`ipc.c` now probes the path before unlinking: a connectable socket means a
live owner and `ipc_init` refuses to bind (compositor starts without IPC);
a dead socket file is unlinked and rebound. A bound unix socket cannot be
re-linked from outside, so recovery without restart requires injecting a
new listener in-process (see below).

### Safe manual launch

```bash
SOMEWM_TEST_NAME=test \
SOMEWM_SOCKET=/run/user/1000/somewm-test/test/ipc.sock \
WAYLAND_DISPLAY=wayland-0 \
somewm -L ~/src/somewm-next/lua
```

All three env vars are required. `SOMEWM_SOCKET` is the one that prevents
socket theft. The test orchestrator (`build/test_orchestrator`, exposed as
`somewm-client test start`) sets all of this up itself
(`test_orchestrator.c:569`) — prefer it where possible.

### Always stop the instance afterwards

A nested instance keeps running until killed, and stray ones accumulate:
they hold a wlr output window on the user's screen, run the full rc.lua
(including autostart spawns), and confuse `screen`/IPC queries. When done:

- orchestrated: `somewm-client test stop --name <name>` (kills the pidfile'd
  process and removes the state dir)
- manual: `kill <pid>` and remove `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/somewm-test/<name>/`
- `somewm-client test list` shows running and stale instances. `(stale)`
  means the pidfile is missing or unreadable — it does NOT mean the
  process is dead. A stale entry can still be a live compositor holding a
  wlr window (seen 2026-11-03: two ~50min-old instances listed stale with
  `?` PIDs). Confirm with `pgrep -ax somewm`, kill by PID, then remove
  the state dir.

### Headless testing — no on-screen window needed

The nested instance does not have to appear on any real screen:

```bash
somewm-client test start --name t --host headless
somewm-client test eval  --name t 'return awesome._test_add_output(1920, 1080)'
somewm-client test stop  --name t
```

`--host headless` sets `WLR_BACKENDS=headless` + `WLR_RENDERER=pixman` —
nothing is displayed anywhere. `awesome._test_add_output(w, h)` hotplugs a
fake output (a real `screen` object appears); it also works on a normal
wayland multi-backend by adding a headless sub-backend on demand. Headless
outputs support `screen scale`, so multi-scale drawin/screenshot tests run
entirely off-screen — e.g. two outputs at 1.0 and 1.25 let a wibox be moved
between scales to verify `d->surface_scale` recreation. Screenshots of
headless screens work normally. Limitations: software (pixman) rendering
only, and no real input devices — use `--host wayland` when pointer/keyboard
injection or a visible window matters.

### run-integration.sh defaults to nested, not headless

`tests/run-integration.sh` reads `HEADLESS=${HEADLESS:-0}` — its header
comment claims headless is the default but the actual default is a nested
window inside the session, and `make test-one` sets nothing. Under nested
the output is whatever size the window happens to be, so
`zwlr_virtual_pointer_v1_motion_absolute` scales the 1280x720 extent every
test passes onto the real layout box: injected clicks and drags land at
the wrong coordinates (~2x off). Pointer-injection tests are only
meaningful with `HEADLESS=1`, where the output really is 1280x720.

## IPC debugging

- Protocol: newline-delimited commands; responses are `STATUS MSG\n[DATA]\n\n`.
  `somewm-client ping` → `OK\nPONG`.
- Client connects but reads hang forever = the listener is bound but the
  event loop never accepts — the main thread is blocked somewhere. Check
  `cat /proc/<pid>/wchan`; `unix_stream_read_generic` means a synchronous
  socket read wedged the loop, `poll_schedule_timeout` means healthy epoll.
- Lua-side dispatch bridge: `objects/ipc.c` (`ipc_dispatch_to_lua`), which
  calls the Lua global `_ipc_dispatch` from `lua/awful/ipc.lua`. A nil Lua
  return means the handler deferred the response itself.
- Emergency re-bind of a stolen/unlinked socket on a live compositor: the
  compositor exposes `org.awesomewm.awful.Remote.Eval` on the session bus;
  call it via the compositor's unique `:1.x` name (use `dbus-send` or
  `busctl --user call <unique-name> /org/awesomewm/awful/Remote
  org.awesomewm.awful.Remote Eval s '<lua>'`). LuaJIT `ffi` is available in
  the Lua state, so a listener can be rebuilt in Lua — see
  `plugins/ipc_rescue.lua` in the user config for a working implementation.

## Widget spec keys are a closed set

`read_tree` (widget.c) only knows the names in its `KEYS()` table, and
some carry hard type contracts: `client`, `surface` and `host` must be a
wayland client/drawable (`read_client` rejects anything else). A spec
that puts e.g. a cairo lgi object under `surface` fails the type check,
the whole tree is refused as malformed, and the drawin "shows nothing"
(maps invisible at 1x1 — seen 2026-11-03 on the resource popup). To pin
Lua objects alongside a spec (e.g. GC-keepalive for `image = surf._native`)
use an unreserved name like `_pin`; unknown keys pass through unread.

## Zombie clients and client_surface()

`globalconf.clients` keeps `Client` objects alive past their surfaces
(Lua-visible state, rules, signal lifetime), so `c->surface.xdg` can be
present while `->surface` is NULL — a teardown-window race. Every bare
`client_surface(c)->` deref is a bet the surface outlives the Client;
guarded code is `!client_has_surface(c)` then a NULL check on the result
(monitor.c:873, window.c resize/setfullscreen — the guards added after
the HDMI-unplug abort, resize <- setmon <- closemon). Timer callbacks
(configure_retry) and commit paths are the other places the same race
hides; iterate-then-deref code over globalconf.clients is the pattern
to audit.

## Hot-reload pitfalls

- During reload, all screens are torn down before `rc.lua` re-executes:
  `#screen == 0`. Anything that touches screens eagerly at module load
  (`naughty.notify`, `awful.popup` construction, `screen.focused()`) can
  crash or assert. Defer via `awful.screen.connect_for_each_screen` or
  guard on `#screen > 0`.
- `awesome.startup` is true during hot reload too (luaa.c ~2840:
  `globalconf.loop == NULL || hot_reload_in_progress`). To distinguish a
  real cold boot from a reload, use `awesome.somewm_ready` — it persists
  across reloads via globalconf.
- A `require` failure is cached, so dependents report a misleading "loop or
  previous error loading module" — find the first error in the log.
- `gears.timer` callbacks run on the main loop. A blocking syscall inside a
  timer freezes the entire compositor.
- The IPC listener can be left stranded by a reload: the socket file is
  recreated but `connect()` gets ECONNREFUSED for new clients while
  pre-reload subscribers hold stale fds (seen 2026-11-03). Restore by
  restarting the compositor, not the session's IPC clients.

## LuaJIT FFI gotcha

`ffi.cdef` with a variadic prototype (`int fcntl(int fd, int cmd, ...)`)
**silently drops** the vararg: `fcntl(fd, F_SETFL, O_NONBLOCK)` returns 0
but applies nothing. Declare it non-variadic (`int fcntl(int fd, int cmd,
int arg)`) — the ABI is identical and the flag actually reaches the kernel.
This caused a real deadlock (blocking `read()` inside a `gears.timer`).

## Git workflow

- One fix per branch, one branch per PR. Don't let unrelated work (other
  sessions' edits, drive-by fixes) ride along on a branch that already has
  an open PR — it drags in unreviewed changes and confuses the reviewer.
- Before committing on a fix branch, check `git status`/`git diff --stat`
  for files unrelated to that fix. Stash them out (`git stash push -- <files>`)
  rather than committing everything staged.

## Useful paths

- Build: `make` / `ninja -C build somewm` (leave 2 cores free)
- `test-render` links `render.c` standalone without the compositor
  objects, so render.c must not reference `somewm_api.h` symbols
  (`some_get_event_loop` et al.): pass runtime services in through the
  render API instead (see `render_cursor_anim`'s `loop` parameter).
- Session introspection client: `~/src/somewm-next/build-asan/somewm-client`
  (`eval`, `client info`/`client list`, `clay tree`, `test ...`)
- Logs from manual nested launches conventionally go to `/tmp/somewm-test-launch*.log`
- Test instance state dir: `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/somewm-test/<name>/`

## Runtime probes and signals (node model)

- `awesome._test_redeclare()` marks every output dirty and runs one
  synchronous frame; returns the scene mutation count. A second call with
  nothing changed must return 0.
- `awesome._test_frame_count` reads `globalconf.frame_commit_count` —
  commits that actually presented. The cheap probe for "is a render-side
  clock driving repaints": it advances with zero declares.
- `awesome.bench_stats()`/`bench_reset()` exist only in `-Dbench=true`
  builds (meson option → `SOMEWM_BENCH`; `make build-bench` →
  `build-bench/`). Probe `type(awesome.bench_stats)` first — a normal
  `build/` compositor returns nil. `make bench-run-live` targets the live
  session but needs the live binary itself to be bench-enabled.
- Solved drawin geometry emits `property::width`/`height`/`x`/`y` on the
  **drawin** object only — `drawin_moveresize` writes the drawable's
  geometry fields directly for solved moves ("solved sizes only publish",
  objects/drawin.c). Watch `w.drawin`, never `w._drawable`, for
  solved-size changes.
- `root.cursor(name)` defers when the pointer is over a surface (the
  wallpaper counts). To force a themed cursor in a test: give a wibox
  `cursor = "watch"` at construction and move the pointer over it with
  `mouse.coords{x=,y=}` — the name applies on motion. `w.cursor=` after
  construction may not reach the drawin property; set it on the drawin or
  in the constructor. Cursor names are sticky across hover-leave until the
  next hover context resolves.
- `drawin.attachment` (objects/drawin.c) is the widget-anchor mechanism:
  kind 1 popup / 2 tooltip / 3 launcher / 4 notification, target =
  widget identity + occurrence + host drawable, resolved C-side to an
  `ELEMENT_WITH_ID` float in `declare_attachment`. There is no Lua-side
  fit/flip: the first preferred position/anchor is used unconditionally.

## Startup cursor position and selmon

At cold boot `monitor_arm_startup_centre()` (monitor.c) warps the cursor
to the centre of the primary screen's monitor and opens a ~10s settle
window (`globalconf.startup_centre_pending`). Inside the window every
`updatemons()` re-asserts that position, so an output-manager apply that
lands moments after login (kanshi's profile) cannot strand the pointer on
whatever output its old coordinates happened to end up inside. The first
real pointer motion (`time != 0` in motionnotify) or the deadline closes
the window. In tests, `root.fake_input("motion_notify")` counts as real
motion and disarms it.

`selmon` tracks the cursor: motionnotify (real motion),
`some_set_cursor_position` (warps), root button presses, `focusclient`,
and `updatemons`'s tail (layout changes that re-clamp the pointer without
any motion event) all keep it on the cursor's monitor. That monitor is
what `createlayersurface` hands to clients that name no output (rofi).
When wlroots relocates a displaced cursor it lands inside the output it
was on; overlapping final boxes make "which output wins" ambiguous, so
tests that need deterministic cursor placement should keep the point
covered through every intermediate layout state.
